Our experiments demonstrate that VLM2Vec can generate embeddings that are highly effective for a wide range of downstream tasks, including classification, visual question answering, multimodal retrieval, and visual grounding. By leveraging the strengths of vision-language models, VLM2Vec is able to produce embeddings that capture both the visual and textual information present in the input data. This makes it a powerful tool for a variety of applications, such as image and video retrieval, content recommendation, and visual question answering. In summary, our contributions are: (1) MMEB, a benchmark for evaluating the performance of multimodal embedding models across a wide range of tasks, and (2) VLM2Vec, a novel framework for training multimodal embedding models that can generate embeddings for any combination of images and text based on task instructions. We believe that MMEB and VLM2Vec will be valuable resources for researchers and practitioners working on multimodal embedding tasks, and we hope that they will help to advance the state of the art in this field.
Related Work
In this section, we provide a review of existing work on multimodal embedding models and the evaluation of their performance on downstream tasks. We begin by discussing the importance of multimodal embedding models in various applications, such as image and video retrieval, content recommendation, and visual question answering. We then review existing work on multimodal embedding models, including models based on vision-language pretraining and contrastive learning. We also discuss the evaluation of these models on downstream tasks, including classification, visual question answering, and retrieval. Finally, we highlight the limitations of existing evaluation methods and the need for a more comprehensive benchmarking approach.

\subsection{Multimodal Embedding Models}
Multimodal embedding models are a type of machine learning model that can learn representations of data that combine both visual and textual information. These models have gained popularity in recent years due to their ability to capture the complex relationships between images and text, making them useful for a wide range of applications. One important application of multimodal embedding models is in image and video retrieval, where they can be used to search for images or videos based on their visual content or textual descriptions. Another application is in content recommendation, where they can be used to recommend images or videos based on a user's interests or preferences. Multimodal embedding models can also be used for visual question answering, where they can answer questions about images or videos based on their visual content and textual descriptions.

\subsection{Vision-Language Pretraining}
One approach to building multimodal embedding models is through vision-language pretraining. This involves training a model on a large dataset of images and text, such as the Conceptual Captions dataset \cite{arxivID-2201.10005-4}, to learn a joint representation of both modalities. This approach has been used to build models such as CLIP \cite{arxivID-2201.10005-5} and ALIGN \cite{arxivID-2201.10005-6}, which have shown state-of-the-art performance on a variety of downstream tasks.

\subsection{Contrastive Learning}
Another approach to building multimodal embedding models is through contrastive learning. This involves training a model to distinguish between positive and negative pairs of images and text. This approach has been used to build models such as SimCSE \cite{arxivID-2201.10005-7} and BLIP \cite{arxivID-2201.10005-8}, which have shown promising results on a variety of downstream tasks.

\subsection{Evaluation of Multimodal Embedding Models}
The evaluation of multimodal embedding models is an important area of research, as it helps to understand the strengths and limitations of different models and to guide the development of new models. One common evaluation method is to use downstream tasks such as classification, visual question answering, and retrieval to measure the performance of the model. These tasks provide a way to evaluate the model's ability to capture the complex relationships between images and text, and to make accurate predictions based on this information. Other evaluation methods include measuring the model's ability to generate meaningful embeddings for images and text, and to compare the embeddings generated by different models.

\subsection{Limitations of Existing Evaluation Methods}
While existing evaluation methods provide valuable insights into the performance of multimodal embedding models, they also have limitations. One limitation is that they may not capture the full range of tasks and applications for which multimodal embedding models are useful. For example, some evaluation methods may focus primarily on classification tasks, while ignoring other important tasks such as visual question answering or content recommendation. Another limitation is that existing evaluation methods may not provide a comprehensive comparison of different models. For example, some evaluation methods may only compare the performance of models on a small number of tasks, making it difficult to draw meaningful conclusions about their relative strengths and weaknesses. Finally, existing evaluation methods may not take into account the computational efficiency of the models. As the size and complexity of multimodal embedding models continue to grow, it is important to evaluate their performance in terms of both accuracy and efficiency.

\subsection{Need for a More Comprehensive Benchmarking Approach}
Given the limitations of existing evaluation methods, there is a need for a more comprehensive benchmarking approach that can provide a more complete picture of the performance of multimodal embedding models. Such an approach should include a wide range of tasks and applications, and should provide a comprehensive comparison of different models. Additionally, the approach should take into account the computational efficiency of the models, and should be designed to be scalable and adaptable to new tasks and applications. By addressing these limitations, we can better understand the strengths and limitations of different multimodal embedding models, and guide the development of new models that are better suited to a wide range of tasks and applications. <|section_end|>
